 # Hermes Agent
 
+## Managed deployment: choose the runtime first
+
+For this distribution, load `hermes-self-management` with `skill_view` before
+administering Hermes. Its deployment-specific routing takes precedence over the
+standalone commands and home paths below; native feature semantics still apply.
+The `terminal` tool runs in a separate SSH terminal, while the active profile state and
+Hermes process are in Kubernetes. From the project root in the terminal, use
+`python3 hermes.py runtime info`, then execute native administration through
+`python3 hermes.py runtime exec -- hermes --profile PROFILE ...`.
+Use the requested profile explicitly, including `default`. Native profile-aware
+tools and the connected UI already act on the runtime; do not wrap them again.
+If the skill or operations bridge is unavailable, report the missing dependency
+instead of administering a local installation. Paths such as `~/.hermes/config.yaml`
+in this reference describe native state in the runtime, not the SSH terminal project root.
+Desktop-local components belong to the user's Desktop installation; a backend
+plugin installation does not install its Desktop UI component.
+
+The installer and interactive local-process examples below apply to a separate,
+explicitly requested standalone installation, not maintenance of this runtime.
+Do not launch another gateway/dashboard in the SSH terminal or update the active core via
+`hermes update`, local package installation or an in-place source pull. Core
+changes follow pinned upstream + exported patches + CI image + GitOps deployment.
+For MCPs, use the native UI/CLI and executor policy described in
+`hermes-self-management`; do not install their dependencies in the terminal or
+Hermes image. For personal skills, use `skill_manage` in the requested profile;
+Git projection happens automatically. Do not edit past session prompts to reload
+these instructions: start a new session when needed.
+
+
 Hermes Agent is an open-source AI agent framework by Nous Research that runs in your terminal, a native desktop app, messaging platforms, and IDEs. It's in the same category as Claude Code (Anthropic), Codex (OpenAI), and OpenClaw — autonomous coding and task-execution agents that use tool calling to interact with your system. Hermes works with any LLM provider (OpenRouter, Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, DeepSeek, xAI, local models, and 20+ others) and runs on Linux, macOS, Windows, and WSL.
 
